# Localization: no defaults on `t()`

## Why

`defaultValue` (and other inline user-visible fallbacks) make the UI look correct even when a key is **missing** from `en.ts`, `fr.ts`, or both. That masks incomplete translations and typos in key paths. Prefer letting the key surface (or fail visibly in dev) so gaps are fixed in locale files.

## Rule

When calling `t()` from `react-i18next` / `useTranslation()`:

- **Do not** pass `defaultValue` in the options object.
- **Do not** use other call forms that embed fallback copy at the call site (e.g. a second string argument as default text).
- **Do not** wrap `t('key')` with `|| '…'` / `?? '…'` to supply English when the lookup fails.

Every user-facing string must live in locale resources (`src/i18n/locales/en.ts` and every other active language). If a key is missing, add it to the locales — do not paper over it in components.

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — masks missing keys and duplicates source of truth
t('auth.settingsTitle', { defaultValue: 'Settings' })
t('explore.authorPage.loading', { defaultValue: 'Loading author.' })
t('some.key') || 'Fallback'

// ✅ GOOD — key only; strings defined in locale files
t('auth.settingsTitle')
t('explore.authorPage.loading')
```

Interpolation and options that are **data**, not default copy, are fine:

```tsx
// ✅ OK
t('items.count', { count: n })
```

## Note

This rule applies to **i18n `t()`** only. Other APIs may use a prop named `defaultValue` (inputs, My Agenda, etc.) — forbid defaults only on translation calls.
